# Settings for smartclock-sensord.  See `smartclock-sensord --help` and
# docs/sensors.md.
#
# There are no default sensors: name at least one before enabling the
# service.  The rest show the built-in defaults.

# hwmon sensors, NAME=PATH, comma-separated, each PATH a temp<N>_input
# or humidity<N>_input file.  To see this host's:
#   for h in /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*; do echo "$h $(cat $h/name)"; ls $h | grep _input; done
# hwmonN numbers can change across boots, so a glob that matches one
# directory, or a symlink, is safer than a numbered path.
#SMARTCLOCK_SENSORD_HWMON=

# IIO sensors, NAME=CHANNEL, comma-separated, each CHANNEL the channel's
# path less its suffix, such as .../iio:deviceN/in_temp.  To see this
# host's:
#   ls /sys/bus/iio/devices/*/ | grep -E '^in_(temp|humidityrelative|pressure)'
#SMARTCLOCK_SENSORD_IIO=

# TEMPer USB sticks, NAME or NAME=PATH, comma-separated: without a
# path the first stick found that no other process holds, with one the
# stick at that hidraw node, such as a udev link.  The temper
# package's udev rules give the node to group temper, which the
# service is in.
#SMARTCLOCK_SENSORD_TEMPER=

#SMARTCLOCK_SENSORD_EVERY=10
#SMARTCLOCK_SENSORD_LOG=/var/lib/smartclock-sensord/sensors.sqlite
#SMARTCLOCK_SENSORD_SOCKET=/run/smartclock-sensord/socket

# Also listen on TCP, HOST:PORT, for clients on other hosts, which use
# --sensord tcp://HOST:PORT.  Nothing decides who may connect
# there.
#SMARTCLOCK_SENSORD_LISTEN=
